er/id #uuid "ac3c1c02-0023-5c17-816d-cabf2be4cdd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723587} :answer-field/id #uuid "d285c740-5c80-5c77-83ef-25af07b3855b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a433f637-292d-58e0-a7da-1e65d17641d7" :question/problem "![](images/70/705db77de56d47d65f80770a9d1a3a3f27b53f992c88b2351f8a320e251bd52f.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by $C$, with\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$. Determine the **pointwise integrand** obtained from this field and parametrization, with the sign adjusted for orientation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}\\boxed{\\ ?\\ }\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The scalar curl is $1-0=1$. The boundary is positively oriented, so we use its circulation without an extra minus sign.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)=\\langle - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the surviving field component, including the orientation sign, and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the missing pointwise expression is $\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e034b13-5641-5afa-94e2-aa5e09214280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723600} {:answer/id #uuid "52e727de-b7fe-54be-81e0-f2be049e4ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723605} {:answer/id #uuid "6613ca63-81d2-5388-8cfd-5d52f62e7da0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723608} {:answer/id #uuid "6c94c41c-61f2-5af6-bcba-1863bc6310e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723610} {:answer/id #uuid "ec00fd92-90ae-59d0-938c-907f1bce5e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c94c41c-61f2-5af6-bcba-1863bc6310e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723610} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de8840a-d8ad-56bd-a909-ceb52ece474c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1495418b-c7bf-5c05-a102-c8da3af4e9b6" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem in flux form to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle\\sin y+2x,\\ e^x-y\\right\\rangle\n$$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le2,\\ 0\\le y\\le1\\}$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, you are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-1.\n$$\n\nUse\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle and let $C=\\partial D$ be traversed counterclockwise, with outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. The components $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangle $D$ and its boundary are shown below.\n\n![Rectangle from x equals 0 to 2 and y equals 0 to 1](images/ea/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nThe term $\\sin y$ is constant when differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, and $e^x$ is constant when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$. Thus the given derivatives yield divergence $2+(-1)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^2(2-1)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[x]_0^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=[2y]_0^1=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, constant divergence $1$ times the rectangle's area $2\\cdot1$ gives outward flux $2$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c8afda-baaf-5aff-9510-9be7d05be2cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723601} {:answer/id #uuid "31801ace-b1be-58e8-8fa0-1b7e1c599c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723602} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef0bd89-c110-5695-9c7e-aa2aab2cf7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723603} {:answer/id #uuid "5783e742-6733-51d1-bea9-568a62f9ea3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723607} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3f4061-8c6f-5aac-9c41-bfe5a2744f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31801ace-b1be-58e8-8fa0-1b7e1c599c4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723602} :answer-field/id #uuid "f582aaad-6d9b-5001-8424-4558472532da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69a7b93b-e328-5297-a19a-d2739f3bb019" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle e^y+x^2y,\\ \\cos x+xy^2+2y\\right\\rangle\n$$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,1)$, and $(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle and let $C=\\partial D$ be traversed counterclockwise, with outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. The components $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertices give $D=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\ 0\\le y\\le1\\}$, shown below.\n\n![Rectangle from x equals minus 1 to 2 and y equals 0 to 1](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nHere $P=e^y+x^2y$ and $Q=\\cos x+xy^2+2y$. Holding the other variable constant gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2xy,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2xy+2,\n\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4xy+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_{-1}^2(4xy+2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[2x^2y+2x]_{-1}^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1\\bigl((8y+4)-(2y-2)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1(6y+6)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=[3y^2+6y]_0^1=9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth parts contribute: the $4xy$ term integrates to $3$, and constant divergence $2$ contributes $2$ times the rectangle's area $3$, giving $6$. Their sum is $9$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10994c2d-ed49-5f5f-a551-412c9bc9abce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723598} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff26730-cbdf-5d59-a485-0990f43a81e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723604} {:answer/id #uuid "53f6280a-cd3e-5c85-92d7-a599c240efe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723606} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fdb55b-47e3-5094-97d9-11bee980e8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723615} {:answer/id #uuid "df0234ed-5403-5104-88e9-482c105c89c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0fdb55b-47e3-5094-97d9-11bee980e8ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723615} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a93a7f6-6a4a-5212-a3c3-87a414686fc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb157262-14ee-5611-b748-c040f98c5da4" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle\\cos y-x^2,\\ \\sin x+3y\\right\\rangle\n$$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $0\\le x\\le2,\\ -1\\le y\\le1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle and let $C=\\partial D$ be traversed counterclockwise, with outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. The components $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangle $D$ and its boundary are shown below.\n\n![Rectangle from x equals 0 to 2 and y equals minus 1 to 1](images/2f/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nHere $P=\\cos y-x^2$ and $Q=\\sin x+3y$. The cross-variable terms contribute zero to the relevant partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=-2x,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le2,\\ -1\\le y\\le1\\}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^1\\int_0^2(3-2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^1[3x-x^2]_0^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^1(6-4)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=[2y]_{-1}^1=2-(-2)=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inner integral is $2$; the outer integral accounts for the full height $1-(-1)=2$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08743a54-0bf3-5cde-b208-4329f6aa7f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723691} {:answer/id #uuid "26438137-97d7-5d98-9de4-e72d76a4e697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723693} {:answer/id #uuid "65a452c8-d8c3-57d7-bce7-6a4ca586cb40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723701} {:answer/id #uuid "9d8bcc79-e9c9-552b-b315-74b6acd810ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723709} {:answer/id #uuid "e646de97-7363-501a-adff-01190dacb183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e646de97-7363-501a-adff-01190dacb183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723720} :answer-field/id #uuid "33647960-5777-540f-929a-94bd9eafe426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ee7e132-bae0-55cc-b643-8f1f2d98882f" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)},- \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/95/952295caff46f0cb7de608127484a2d5bc2474516df18793c772d67ab7f8c3b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is clockwise. Choose $P=0$, $Q=x$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(\\oint_C x\\,dy\\right).\n$$\nThe minus sign corrects the negative orientation.\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=12 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}3 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(4 t)=2\\sin(2 t)\\cos(2 t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\cos(2 t)$, so $du=-2\\sin(2 t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=-12\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=6\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=2\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=2\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-4\\right)=4.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01bc4242-cd7f-56a1-aec0-f3c9abe6dabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723688} {:answer/id #uuid "05371b20-caed-5f52-b690-b7b6b17064d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723690} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff2e82d-688a-577b-81e1-431d9dcdf389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723696} {:answer/id #uuid "96b667eb-75d3-5b99-97e9-b5ed2196ccf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723708} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd50b1e-06d5-564c-ae05-f698a8a97328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01bc4242-cd7f-56a1-aec0-f3c9abe6dabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723688} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d7c085-7434-53db-933f-5cc473bb21a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723689}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "522f0ac2-2063-525a-b64c-ee4b79e60cb9" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/90/900a1558522e97f234029e84361c00f514091b3d67cbb7373fa3c3faa53b88ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is counterclockwise. Choose $P=0$, $Q=x$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(\\oint_C x\\,dy\\right).\n$$\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\cos(t)$, so $du=-1\\sin(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=24\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=-24\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=-8\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=-8\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(16\\right)=16.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723710 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2f9dc1-6287-5230-ba99-a3a23e0573cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723692} {:answer/id #uuid "457ceb5c-2f6c-5475-b075-8c1983e48b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723698} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce17bc9-7568-5dfc-a28a-153788bf1abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723707} {:answer/id #uuid "bf59b0f9-fb6d-55f2-b2b6-f38c57fb9d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723714} {:answer/id #uuid "cf65fdea-f507-5cee-9160-ba8500a2ba60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf65fdea-f507-5cee-9160-ba8500a2ba60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723716} :answer-field/id #uuid "465abfc0-21d2-55f8-80f4-00c443194423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f4fc6df-e157-59b8-91ad-af3a0c781dd5" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq t\\leq \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/7d/7d9ab5c8f992e07fdfbba34a0e3ed4d3df56fc3f9002912229e75b7f33bf8bb6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is clockwise. Choose $P=-y$, $Q=0$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-\\oint_C y\\,dx\\right).\n$$\nThe minus sign corrects the negative orientation.\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}-\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\sin(t)$, so $du=1\\cos(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=12\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=12\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=4\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=4\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-8\\right)=8.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f78f0cd-243a-5f65-a50c-d050a0e55dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723705} {:answer/id #uuid "ce31dc7f-a0c1-572e-bb19-5cfdc3e34161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723715} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fc5416-8cde-5345-a464-9a64e0941d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723717} {:answer/id #uuid "e1bdbe0e-d00c-5efb-9c29-55797f776e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723719} {:answer/id #uuid "f26c1a7b-d0b1-5a85-8790-f997e8d9b0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce31dc7f-a0c1-572e-bb19-5cfdc3e34161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723715} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aaa6c71-c99a-5089-9297-7d41ac5cd4eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a490eb9d-df64-5d96-9330-adebbe6e7aa0" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq t\\leq \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/6f/6f382bf2e83e21a77c17f98a20d5d62b0efe8f5e1308f037f47032475abf5490.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is counterclockwise. Choose $P=0$, $Q=x$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(\\oint_C x\\,dy\\right).\n$$\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=- 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\sin(t)$, so $du=1\\cos(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=-16\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=-16\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=- \\frac{16}{3}\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=- \\frac{16}{3}\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right)=\\frac{32}{3}.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723718 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69823b75-9121-5a7b-b8a3-57f7ca63c447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723702} {:answer/id #uuid "714708b7-c76d-5288-a6cb-1db9157b74ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723704} {:answer/id #uuid "8a33b235-de78-5c57-9ece-9eefb6577248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723706} {:answer/id #uuid "a55c034a-b737-5da0-bfda-f78afc75000a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723712} {:answer/id #uuid "b04b26bd-f06d-5b01-9c5c-0f160f268f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a55c034a-b737-5da0-bfda-f78afc75000a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723712} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fd5db7-ab09-579f-a9a6-a357c1f2713f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dee8cf91-3b7b-5b7a-a537-4103dbba8768" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/90/906f41119591337834b399bdad98dd3484878d5913f0aaddfbc2bf62d04b90f2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is clockwise. Choose $P=-y$, $Q=0$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-\\oint_C y\\,dx\\right).\n$$\nThe minus sign corrects the negative orientation.\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=- 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}-\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\cos(t)$, so $du=-1\\sin(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=-12\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=12\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=4\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=4\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-8\\right)=8.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9ccd82-ac1e-5f89-bb13-738e7c1a4d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723728} {:answer/id #uuid "221c0947-ed73-577d-8ae7-e1209711fc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723729} {:answer/id #uuid "53576bdb-9ae9-5928-bc2e-8b63a09d97d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723736} {:answer/id #uuid "73dc39e9-0ae9-51a9-8795-9c1e0f96d2c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723738} {:answer/id #uuid "c6117e68-b5f4-5caa-a2ca-348d562d190c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221c0947-ed73-577d-8ae7-e1209711fc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723729} :answer-field/id #uuid "926793cc-d1e0-5437-b8cc-fca03845dd52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bcb0e97-ed4e-5c4e-aa70-e775666c20db" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\;- y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region between $y=0$ and $y=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=2 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=- y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=1.\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\geq0$. Use vertical slices from $y=0$ to $y=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/3e/3e25c6423bcef348cfd0ac91b32d4d65c3ae980def82d8e220d5bff659193936.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\left(1\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[- 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(-2\\right) \\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47970dc2-699c-59bc-841c-a39bcc213dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{128}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723735} {:answer/id #uuid "6259e9fa-7253-5a85-a6a8-173b2c7b432c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723737} {:answer/id #uuid "91c8ba26-f4de-57d4-a582-371f4203ec6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723744} {:answer/id #uuid "a948ff59-e3bc-52e8-b7f8-d074ce3487ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723747} {:answer/id #uuid "cffa6189-44cb-5d41-bea5-09cc6f81c433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{32 \\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a948ff59-e3bc-52e8-b7f8-d074ce3487ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723747} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9e277d-7260-569e-a23e-749319a7d5b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "355d5866-e826-55c2-b385-9f45b916864f" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 2 x^{2} y - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\;e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=2 \\sqrt{x}$, and $x=2$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- 2 x^{2} y - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=e^{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=- 4 x y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=- 4 x y.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=2$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=2 \\sqrt{x}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/a1/a1c6dfa6dd998451b9caf7772e1b1931a780cc689c8fa301916c7498adf005f2.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 2,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 \\sqrt{x}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{x}}\\left(- 4 x y\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[- 2 x y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 \\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[\\left(- 8 x^{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(- 8 x^{2}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[- \\frac{8 x^{3}}{3}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\&=\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=- \\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $- \\frac{64}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38b2b23b-9513-5111-989b-fae8ef285d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723731} {:answer/id #uuid "39641ad9-da02-5ea1-b83e-e8d62f867047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723733} {:answer/id #uuid "43edae46-4148-5e5b-a6e0-e6c81b373ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723734} {:answer/id #uuid "b53094ec-b48a-5349-a2f7-6efd89cccd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723749} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c8b20a-e2b4-56ee-bff4-e9dcecd68441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b53094ec-b48a-5349-a2f7-6efd89cccd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723749} :answer-field/id #uuid "deaecbc9-2a26-5256-9a66-5ccf775038f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ddafa9e-38e4-5d0a-8ab8-113ae1236217" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - x + y,\\;x^{2} - y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region between $y=0$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- x + y$ and $Q=x^{2} - y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=-1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=-2.\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\geq0$. Use vertical slices from $y=0$ to $y=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/82/8253ae5644e9229ad0fd91023cd296b7dfca96f1d089d4e776c496898983737d.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\left(-2\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[- 2 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(2\\right) \\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175e4073-d322-5f74-b77b-419aab078966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723727} {:answer/id #uuid "393339ef-34ee-5047-8bdd-b0b02a94c209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723732} {:answer/id #uuid "830d62e1-b875-50dc-bd6d-474b66081e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723740} {:answer/id #uuid "891c0765-d5b5-5a78-9403-af38ff28dde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723741} {:answer/id #uuid "f6eea517-d754-5896-98d4-cca38e3e2f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "891c0765-d5b5-5a78-9403-af38ff28dde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723741} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffffa75e-2d10-567f-aef4-f3155c71c6b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab4c70ec-18c6-59b5-9423-196d95c46283" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\;2 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region between $y=0$ and $y=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=3 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=2 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=3,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=5.\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\geq0$. Use vertical slices from $y=0$ to $y=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/78/786e6657e805e8c3968dbd64180e4d0405254c7649fc8213598a803f05ad0d19.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}\\left(5\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[5 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\left(10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[10 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{x=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\&=\\left(10\\right)-\\left(-10\\right) \\\\&=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $20$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02289a4a-2d17-5203-93c2-ef4c4080fec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723724} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2c0d8f-91bb-5314-9eda-d976064442ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723726} {:answer/id #uuid "7efd0613-8b29-5a0d-83a2-6082dd38e8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723739} {:answer/id #uuid "8abdbfa7-0096-5f5f-a170-0b79d3e4c625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723742} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c7a2fc-1a9a-5275-9805-4a8daa84eb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e2c0d8f-91bb-5314-9eda-d976064442ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723726} :answer-field/id #uuid "96594be5-6d15-5d12-bf80-e9d5c515bb72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed7c8fee-e3fd-522a-8e3c-75dab9244930" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x^{2} + y,\\;x^{2} - 3 x y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=x^{2}$, and $x=2$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=3 x^{2} + y$ and $Q=x^{2} - 3 x y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=6 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=- 3 x,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3 x.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=2$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=x^{2}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/5b/5b29e3b24bc18633df2ab03028bfd2449e7eaf4b9f2dda0a3ab78b0ea366008c.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 2,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[3 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{2}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[\\left(3 x^{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(3 x^{3}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[\\frac{3 x^{4}}{4}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\&=\\left(12\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $12$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010da69a-8d5f-524a-9d91-6ae700a3d361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723760} {:answer/id #uuid "29b49ec7-b998-58c9-99e7-93ddea631c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723766} {:answer/id #uuid "683d639d-38e9-5a3d-acb2-8a2a4eb79f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723773} {:answer/id #uuid "757f68b0-4122-5084-884f-0b2d3d0f742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723775} {:answer/id #uuid "ae26ab0e-4fcf-53e3-a34e-7e91220e8e72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "010da69a-8d5f-524a-9d91-6ae700a3d361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723760} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b298af0-371d-5b6d-83ad-8f46d6c2ad49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02192e56-a7e3-5a1e-ab9f-075c60f85eb7" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle x + y,\\;x^{2} + 2 y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(-4,2)$, and $(4,2)$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 2,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/f1/f1808908b559896a7a9cdc76067304d66a52e6eb58a250fe4164e14b27085d27.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x + y$ and $Q=x^{2} + 2 y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3.\n$$\n\nThe lower vertex has $y=0$ and the top edge has $y=2$. Horizontal slices run between the two sloping sides, from $x=- 2 y$ to $x=2 y$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/f1/f1808908b559896a7a9cdc76067304d66a52e6eb58a250fe4164e14b27085d27.png)\n\nThis is a type II region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 2,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{- 2 y}^{2 y}\\left(3\\right)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[3 x\\right]_{x=- 2 y}^{x=2 y}\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\right]\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(12 y\\right)\\,dy \\\\&=\\left[6 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\&=\\left(24\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $24$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723768 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a7e8e6-e832-53a6-ab74-fbee659fbb30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723765} {:answer/id #uuid "422ebd9e-31fd-5836-a8cb-a631b9197572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723770} {:answer/id #uuid "822fca52-395f-5476-9cdc-a9f328693676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723776} {:answer/id #uuid "9dece2f8-dd84-519d-be49-ac8aa1fbd0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723778} {:answer/id #uuid "dad23aba-1814-5cd1-b5c9-89f181237c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "422ebd9e-31fd-5836-a8cb-a631b9197572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723770} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5fd2f19-d1cf-56a0-93da-369f07829a39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d2569e3-4f58-5a39-956f-dbf2e5f93dd7" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle x - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\;y + e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=x^{5}$, and $x=1$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{5}\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/89/89edc4d1c65965d1d423115458d583001e73f18554950d2d85d04b75ac002c96.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=y + e^{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=1$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=x^{5}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/89/89edc4d1c65965d1d423115458d583001e73f18554950d2d85d04b75ac002c96.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{5}}\\left(2\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[2 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{5}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[\\left(2 x^{5}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 x^{5}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[\\frac{x^{6}}{3}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $\\frac{1}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06587231-2d4b-569b-affc-0aeba59d69d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723762} {:answer/id #uuid "15219215-b859-5330-946a-d7e634d18fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723764} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa65d24-768e-5de7-8b99-8d4deeab08ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723769} {:answer/id #uuid "51d170d7-47bd-5b93-9f57-6412fa0ab316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723771} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2d39c4-4402-53e2-a3e5-f2a6de71f22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51d170d7-47bd-5b93-9f57-6412fa0ab316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723771} :answer-field/id #uuid "8743441e-40c0-53b2-a4b0-e352e2c07699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "710917fb-a6b7-5f5a-803d-34d1863398b1" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x + y,\\;x^{2} + y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(-2,1)$, and $(2,1)$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 1,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/dd/dde0c524fe9351f830a5daf49e507142650cdd854bdd52744a3b21208634933f.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=2 x + y$ and $Q=x^{2} + y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3.\n$$\n\nThe lower vertex has $y=0$ and the top edge has $y=1$. Horizontal slices run between the two sloping sides, from $x=- 2 y$ to $x=2 y$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/dd/dde0c524fe9351f830a5daf49e507142650cdd854bdd52744a3b21208634933f.png)\n\nThis is a type II region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 1,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{- 2 y}^{2 y}\\left(3\\right)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[3 x\\right]_{x=- 2 y}^{x=2 y}\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\right]\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 y\\right)\\,dy \\\\&=\\left[6 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\&=\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d4d8731-731f-5d79-b956-5ea953abe916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723772} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6470d4-1cb4-551b-a48b-0c31fc9bb0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723779} {:answer/id #uuid "a851c538-dd38-5669-9a75-597f114a2bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723780} {:answer/id #uuid "ceff3e2b-b2ee-5794-9488-b6efb417c0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723783} {:answer/id #uuid "dafdb55f-8d28-5473-bdac-1896a81da6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dafdb55f-8d28-5473-bdac-1896a81da6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723785} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a2a6912-2d28-5d6a-9b43-ac47a67c4bdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8edf0c6-6f4c-5f8a-8d54-195901ecbd11" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 2 x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\;- x y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=2 x^{3}$, and $x=1$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 x^{3}\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/7f/7ff77b6a682420607a6188c73de2940a434c4ce920b4e15e7e0e7d531e3bfc16.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- 2 x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=- x y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=- 4 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=- x,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=- 5 x.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=1$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=2 x^{3}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/7f/7ff77b6a682420607a6188c73de2940a434c4ce920b4e15e7e0e7d531e3bfc16.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 x^{3}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2 x^{3}}\\left(- 5 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[- 5 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 x^{3}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[\\left(- 10 x^{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 10 x^{4}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[- 2 x^{5}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f16c26b-4a38-5d34-9326-d4f8e3effa7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{-2}\\int_{0}^{2}(8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723791} {:answer/id #uuid "58d2c6f7-acc6-5c6f-9e9c-969ba4cc40ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{2}^{0}(2 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723798} {:answer/id #uuid "af804953-06ef-5b0d-879d-693b692dd629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}(8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723805} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcdec30-3141-5101-97a0-58d2e69fac9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{2}^{0}(8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723812} {:answer/id #uuid "e42b6554-85db-5f6f-bef3-75e29da765ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}(2 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af804953-06ef-5b0d-879d-693b692dd629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}(8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723805} :answer-field/id #uuid "672b8aaa-d130-5678-9067-0bc8afd6f93a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cda95c6-ba9e-5299-9819-e6c0d3386c73" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(0,-2)$, $(2,-2)$, $(2,0)$, $(0,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle 3 x y^{3},\\ 2 x y^{4}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=3 y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=8 x y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(3 y^{3})+(8 x y^{3})\\\\ &=8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 2,\\ -2\\leq y\\leq 0\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/41/4174dc632b4b98c4c2650e42101b10ffbd28836e09d9ffd042c53812855376ba.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}(8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723795 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a95479f-e57c-5149-8c3f-a020b0981901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{-3}^{-1}(2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723792} {:answer/id #uuid "3861e62d-9ae9-5579-bab4-af05061ac3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\int_{-3}^{0}(- 2 x y^{2} + 2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723793} {:answer/id #uuid "ca124483-5609-5778-9417-b39ccfd818a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\int_{-3}^{0}(2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723811} {:answer/id #uuid "f8622f22-49c6-5476-9995-039fd421124d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{-3}(- x y^{2} + 2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723819} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ba8031-e3a6-5bc4-9f04-217f1ce77350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\int_{0}^{-3}(- 2 x y^{2} + 2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3861e62d-9ae9-5579-bab4-af05061ac3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\int_{-3}^{0}(- 2 x y^{2} + 2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723793} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bbbc214-00e5-5cb3-94a8-40f8380558dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4617f21a-f078-569f-9899-b114b63e0b48" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(-3,-3)$, $(-1,-3)$, $(-1,0)$, $(-3,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - x^{2} y^{2},\\ 2 x y\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 2 x y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 2 x y^{2})+(2 x)\\\\ &=- 2 x y^{2} + 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-3\\leq x\\leq -1,\\ -3\\leq y\\leq 0\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/43/432f70bfcb8fbd0d0edec65352a8c286be3f3a2ca4becce569f2e8d912d03aa6.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\int_{-3}^{0}(- 2 x y^{2} + 2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0529af8d-2432-529a-8bda-7343fb0d8623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{2}^{-1}(12 x y^{3} - y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723788} {:answer/id #uuid "b110047d-31f8-5104-adb4-7de1f48caf26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{2}^{-1}(12 x y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723806} {:answer/id #uuid "b3171669-df61-50d9-b5a3-32210d54cef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{-1}^{2}(12 x y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723807} {:answer/id #uuid "c816b191-7bf7-5e6b-b4ce-277ce34c171f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{-1}\\int_{-1}^{2}(12 x y^{3} - y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723809} {:answer/id #uuid "e500f3d6-a883-50a5-ba56-f8e5f1dc62ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{-1}^{2}(12 x y^{3} - y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e500f3d6-a883-50a5-ba56-f8e5f1dc62ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{-1}^{2}(12 x y^{3} - y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723815} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf8d808-93cd-52e2-8aec-476a4c74d178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48f17d31-0683-5ff7-b622-99b414c231f0" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1)$, $(0,-1)$, $(0,2)$, $(-1,2)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - x y^{3},\\ 3 x y^{4}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=12 x y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- y^{3})+(12 x y^{3})\\\\ &=12 x y^{3} - y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\leq x\\leq 0,\\ -1\\leq y\\leq 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/26/26d43b65f45dfa30023a33369d5c2d184641a3e5f83e63e251078685f5328dc6.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{-1}^{2}(12 x y^{3} - y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f0d0fe5-fed5-5797-9711-d214e8b590fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{2}^{1}(- 16 x y^{6})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\