epeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 2 y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=8 x y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 2 y^{3})+(8 x y^{3})\\\\ &=8 x y^{3} - 2 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-3\\leq x\\leq 0,\\ 1\\leq y\\leq 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/ad/ad86eea1220a388c4f2240f4279dd2e6f7e31ad3d31a0dcc22090e3572ab3c3a.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{1}^{2}(8 x y^{3} - 2 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723803 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "433fe902-f4b7-59e0-8aa2-32d82c9e6e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{-2}^{0}(- 2 x^{2} - 9 x y^{2} + y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723794} {:answer/id #uuid "5dcb6a54-40cc-5a2a-8220-b97f69446e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{-3}^{0}(- 2 x^{2} - 9 x y^{2} + y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723799} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1f97bd-8ee0-5236-8000-73f986435d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{0}^{-2}(- 2 x y - 11 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723800} {:answer/id #uuid "d62f657c-94e7-5586-83a1-77137650f0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{-2}^{0}(2 x^{2} + 9 x y^{2} + y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723813} {:answer/id #uuid "f7681229-10d0-55e2-8c6b-b9331e052c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{-2}^{0}(- 2 x y - 11 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7681229-10d0-55e2-8c6b-b9331e052c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{-2}^{0}(- 2 x y - 11 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723818} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f3a17e-7141-5811-8098-3249e06637c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94992f22-ed25-57cd-b3f5-cc09fab9acd4" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(-2,-3)$, $(0,-3)$, $(0,0)$, $(-2,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - 2 x^{2} y - 3 x y^{3},\\ x y^{2} - 2 y^{4}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 4 x y - 3 y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 x y - 8 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 4 x y - 3 y^{3})+(2 x y - 8 y^{3})\\\\ &=- 2 x y - 11 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\leq x\\leq 0,\\ -3\\leq y\\leq 0\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/b8/b89a4729afdaf568863a36b2d98b478c9ceb637a7eee2cdd8ee9168477b5c592.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{-2}^{0}(- 2 x y - 11 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "539240c7-0a83-5360-a424-4dc334ec1884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}^{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}(6 x^{2} + 24 x y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723836} {:answer/id #uuid "568036e6-f163-5f40-be22-3d34b190d8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}^{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}(- 7 x - 4 y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723837} {:answer/id #uuid "b4090e2d-e25f-5abb-b6fb-15317b2d5774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}^{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}(6 x^{2} + 24 x y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723844} {:answer/id #uuid "b6254167-2c06-5bd3-b55e-d573f037538d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{3}^{-1}\\int_{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}^{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}(- 7 x - 4 y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723845} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f4199d-a89c-57e8-a903-ff1421cf4191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}^{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}(- 7 x - 4 y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "568036e6-f163-5f40-be22-3d34b190d8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}^{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}(- 7 x - 4 y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723837} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20721f3-9040-5927-8d1a-a5c0163469dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186723842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ce9d227-1177-5c0a-8ab5-04fef149133f" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $x=- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14$ and $x=2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - 3 x^{2} - 3 y^{3},\\ - x y - 2 y^{2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 6 x\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- x - 4 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 6 x)+(- x - 4 y)\\\\ &=- 7 x - 4 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2&=- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14\\\\ 4\\left(y - 1\\right)^2&=16\\\\ y&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad y=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $x=2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2$ and the upper boundary is $x=- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1&\\leq y\\leq 3,\\\\ 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2&\\leq x\\leq - 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/0e/0ec4207925a9b0019e058d5a35b3cf14f5face57c304291ecb624808984469f5.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}^{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}(- 7 x - 4 y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}]}
